---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const pre = "font-mono text-sm bg-muted p-3 rounded overflow-x-auto";

const ruleExample = `{
  "type": "forbidden",
  "id": "LAYER-CONTROLLER-REPO-001",
  "from": ["src/**/*.controller.ts"],
  "to": ["src/**/*.repository.ts"],
  "message": "Controllers call services, not repositories."
}`;

const typeOnlyExample = `import type { User } from './user';        // erased: never runs
import { UserService } from './user.service';  // loads, if used as a value`;

const cycleExample = `Component cycles (1):
  src/orders → src/users → src/orders
    src/orders/orders.controller.ts:2:33  '@users/repositories/users.repository' → src/users/repositories/users.repository.ts
    src/users/users.service.ts:3:31  '../orders/orders.service' → src/orders/orders.service.ts`;

const sections = [
  { id: "rules", title: "Rules as Fitness Functions" },
  { id: "graph", title: "The Import Graph" },
  { id: "runtime", title: "Runtime and Type-Only Imports" },
  { id: "components", title: "Components and Packages" },
  { id: "coupling", title: "Coupling and Instability" },
  { id: "abstractness", title: "Abstractness" },
  { id: "distance", title: "Distance" },
  { id: "cycles", title: "Cycles" },
  { id: "baselines", title: "Baselines" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Concepts - tdepend"
  description="How tdepend builds its import graph, groups files into components, measures coupling, finds cycles and uses baselines"
>
  <section class="py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
      <div class="text-center space-y-4 mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Concepts</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground">
          The ideas behind tdepend, and what its numbers mean
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col lg:flex-row gap-12">
        <div class="flex-1 min-w-0">
          <div class="space-y-12">
            <div id="rules" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Rules as Fitness Functions</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p>
                  A fitness function is an automated check that keeps an
                  architecture aligned with its intended design. In tdepend the
                  fitness functions are <strong>rules</strong>: statements about
                  which imports are allowed.
                </p>
                <pre class={pre}>{ruleExample}</pre>
                <p>
                  <code>tdepend check</code> evaluates every rule and exits 1
                  when an import breaks one, so the rule holds on every pull
                  request. There are four rule types: <code>forbidden</code>,
                  <code>layers</code>, <code>private</code> and
                  <code>no-cycles</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  A rule points at one import to fix. That is why rules can fail
                  a build and metrics cannot: a number such as distance tells
                  you where to look, not what to change.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="graph" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">The Import Graph</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p>
                  Everything rests on one graph: which file imports which.
                  tdepend builds it with TypeScript's own module resolver, using
                  each file's nearest <code>tsconfig</code>. Path aliases,
                  package <code>exports</code>, project references and workspace
                  packages resolve exactly as they do for the compiler.
                </p>
                <p>
                  A workspace package is followed to its source files, whether
                  or not it has been built. Every import is also given a class,
                  such as <code>internal</code>, <code>external</code> or
                  <code>unresolved</code>, so nothing disappears silently.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Runtime and Type-Only Imports</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p>
                  Not every import runs. The compiler removes imports that are
                  only used as types, so they can't cause a runtime cycle.
                </p>
                <pre class={pre}>{typeOnlyExample}</pre>
                <p>
                  tdepend decides this for each import, and it is conservative:
                  an import counts as loading its target unless tsc, esbuild and
                  swc would all drop it. So an import reported as erased is
                  erased by every one of them.
                  <code>tdepend explain &lt;file&gt;</code> shows the verdict
                  and the reason for each import in a file.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="components" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Components and Packages</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p>
                  Architecture is about groups of files, not single files. A
                  <strong>component</strong> is such a group: a folder matched
                  by a pattern like <code>src/*</code>, or a NestJS module
                  folder. A <strong>package</strong> is a workspace package.
                </p>
                <p>
                  tdepend aggregates the file graph to both levels. Rules,
                  metrics and cycles can then be read per file, per component
                  or per package. Files that belong to no component are
                  collected in a <code>(root)</code> group.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="coupling" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Coupling and Instability</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <ul class="space-y-2 ml-4">
                  <li>
                    • <strong>Afferent coupling (Ca):</strong> how many others
                    depend on this one
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    • <strong>Efferent coupling (Ce):</strong> how many others
                    this one depends on
                  </li>
                </ul>
                <p class="font-mono text-sm bg-muted p-3 rounded">
                  Instability = Ce / (Ca + Ce)
                </p>
                <p>
                  An instability near 0 means many things depend on the
                  component and it depends on little, so it is costly to
                  change. Near 1 means the opposite: it is free to change.
                  Neighbours are counted at the level being measured, and npm
                  packages are not counted.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="abstractness" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Abstractness</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p class="font-mono text-sm bg-muted p-3 rounded">
                  Abstractness = abstract declarations / counted declarations
                </p>
                <p>
                  In Java an interface is an abstraction. In TypeScript most
                  interfaces only describe data. So by default tdepend counts
                  an interface or abstract class as abstract
                  <strong>only when something implements or extends it</strong>,
                  and it leaves fields-only declarations such as DTOs out of
                  the ratio altogether.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Set <code>metrics.abstractness</code> to
                  <code>"jdepend"</code> for the classic definition, where
                  every interface, type alias and abstract class is abstract.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="distance" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Distance</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p class="font-mono text-sm bg-muted p-3 rounded">
                  Distance = | Abstractness + Instability − 1 |
                </p>
                <p>
                  The main sequence is the line where the two balance: a
                  component is either stable and abstract, or unstable and
                  concrete. Distance is how far a component sits from that
                  line.
                </p>
                <ul class="space-y-2 ml-4">
                  <li>
                    • <strong>Zone of pain:</strong> stable and concrete (A
                    and I near 0). Many depend on it, and it is hard to extend.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    • <strong>Zone of uselessness:</strong> abstract and
                    unstable (A and I near 1). Abstractions nobody depends on.
                  </li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                  Distance is a signal, not a verdict.
                  <code>metrics.thresholds.distance</code> lists the components
                  beyond a value in the <code>analyze</code> report. It never
                  changes the exit code.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="cycles" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Cycles</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p>
                  A cycle is a set of files, components or packages that all
                  reach each other through imports. tdepend finds them with
                  Tarjan's strongly connected components algorithm, which runs
                  in O(V + E) time.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Knowing that twenty files form a cycle doesn't tell you what
                  to fix. So each cycle is reported with its shortest loop and
                  the exact import behind every step:
                </p>
                <pre class={pre}>{cycleExample}</pre>
                <p>
                  By default only imports that run are considered, so a loop
                  made of type-only imports is not a cycle. Cycles make
                  components impossible to test or change in isolation, and can
                  cause initialisation-order bugs.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="border-t"></div>

            <div id="baselines" class="scroll-mt-20 space-y-4">
              <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Baselines</h2>
              <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-3 leading-relaxed">
                <p>
                  A new rule usually finds violations that already exist. A
                  baseline records them, with a count, so the rule can be
                  enforced from today: existing violations are accepted, and
                  only new ones fail the build.
                </p>
                <p>
                  A violation is matched by rule, file, target and specifier,
                  not by line number, so editing a file doesn't disturb it.
                  <code>tdepend baseline --prune</code> shrinks the baseline as
                  violations are fixed, and never accepts a new one.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="hidden lg:block lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:h-fit w-56 shrink-0">
          <div class="rounded-lg border bg-muted/50 p-4 space-y-2">
            <div
              class="text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wide mb-4"
            >
              On this page
            </div>
            <nav class="space-y-1">
              {
                sections.map((section) => (
                  <a
                    href={`#${section.id}`}
                    class="block text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-primary transition-colors py-1.5 px-2 rounded hover:bg-muted"
                  >
                    {section.title}
                  </a>
                ))
              }
            </nav>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
